# Build output
build/
cmake-build-*/
out/
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
CMakeUserPresets.json
cmake_install.cmake
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.so
*.dylib
*.dll
*.exe
# Anchored to the repo root so a future source dir or asset named
# `hxediter` (e.g. src/hxediter/) isn't silently swallowed. Only the
# in-tree-build binaries are the intended target — actual release
# builds land in build/ and are already covered above.
/hxediter
/hxsort
/hxediter-updater-helper
*.exp
*.pdb
*.ilk

# IDE / editor
.idea/
.vscode/
.vs/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.claude/

# Dear ImGui runtime state
imgui.ini

# OS cruft
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
# Apple metadata that Finder writes as separate ._* files on non-HFS
# filesystems (USB sticks, network shares, this repo when checked out
# on a Mac) — defensive even on a Windows-targeted branch.
._*
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride

# Secrets / signing — never commit credentials or signing material.
# Highest-risk on Windows is *.pfx: signtool's PKCS#12 archive holds
# the Authenticode private key, so one stray *.pfx in the working tree
# is enough for an attacker to sign binaries as you. *.p12 / *.p8 are
# the cross-platform / Apple-notarization equivalents, listed here in
# case anyone cross-builds the macOS port from this branch.
*.pfx
*.p12
*.p8
*.pem
*.key
*.cer
*.crt
.env
.env.*
.netrc
id_rsa
id_ed25519

# Per-release artifacts from the updater release workflow
SHA256SUMS.txt
# Stray diagnostic build outputs (e.g. from PowerShell compile tests)
test.obj
hello.cpp
winhello.cpp
*.log
